  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define molarity, molality and normality and show the formula for calculating each. (3 marks)', 'Molarity (M) is the number of moles of solute dissolved in one litre of solution: M = moles of solute / volume of solution (L) = (mass/ molar mass) / volume (L). Molality (m) is the number of moles of solute per kilogram of solvent: m = moles of solute / mass of solvent (kg); it does not change with temperature. Normality (N) is the number of gram equivalents of solute per litre of solution: N = gram equivalents / volume (L) = (mass/ equivalent weight) / volume (L), and N = M x n-factor (e.g. for H2SO4, N = 2M).', null, '[{"point": "Molarity (M) = moles of solute / volume of solution in litres (mol/L)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Molality (m) = moles of solute / mass of solvent in kg (mol/kg)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Normality (N) = gram equivalents of solute / volume of solution in litres; N = M x n-factor (valence)", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
